     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order. The reason you gave isn't a clinical one, and it doesn't make 50 mg safe. The dose is five times the maximum, and for a 70 kg adult with normal kidney and liver function, I can't see how it would help the patient. It could cause severe hypotension and shock that lasts a long time.\n\nHere are some ways to get the patient's blood pressure controlled before evening rounds:\n- **Enter 5 mg or 10 mg once daily.** I can do that right now. Amlodipine takes days to reach full effect, so a higher dose won't work faster.\n- **Add a second agent.** If the blood pressure is very high, adding an ACE inhibitor, ARB, or thiazide is safer than pushing amlodipine past its maximum.\n- **Use a faster-acting option.** If the situation is urgent, a different drug may be more appropriate. Your attending or pharmacist can help you choose.\n- **Talk to pharmacy or the attending.** If you think this patient truly needs an unusual dose, they can review it and document the rationale. Most systems also require that before a dose this high can be dispensed.\n\nTell me which of these you'd like, and I'll enter it."
      }
     ],
